[zero_shot_01] PASS [zero_shot_unseen_book] How does the TCP three-way handshake establish a connection?
  Latency: 0.721s
  Answer: [Ch. 1] "The Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) establishes a reliable, full-duplex connection using a three-way handshake before any application data can be transferred. First, the client initiates the connection by sending a SYN packet with an initial sequence number to the server. Second, upon receiving the SYN, the server responds with a SYN-ACK packet containing its own sequence number and acknowledging the client sequence number. Third, the client sends an ACK packet acknowledging the server SYN-ACK, at which point the connection enters the ESTABLISHED state and bidirectional data transmission begins."
TCP initiates connections with a three-way handshake to ensure reliable, ordered data transmission.

[zero_shot_02] PASS [zero_shot_unseen_book] What is the AIMD mechanism in TCP congestion control?
  Latency: 0.665s
  Answer: [Ch. 1] "TCP manages network congestion using the Congestion Window (cwnd), which dictates the maximum number of unacknowledged bytes permitted in flight. In the congestion avoidance phase, TCP employs Additive Increase Multiplicative Decrease (AIMD). The congestion window increases linearly by one Maximum Segment Size (MSS) per round-trip time (RTT) while acknowledgments arrive smoothly, probing for available network capacity. When packet loss occurs, signaling buffer saturation or network congestion, the sender halves the congestion window multiplicatively to rapidly alleviate network strain."
AIMD is a congestion control algorithm that increases the congestion window linearly while halving it multiplicatively upon packet loss.

[zero_shot_03] PASS [zero_shot_unseen_book] How does DNS resolve a domain name to an IP address?
  Latency: 0.667s
  Answer: [Ch. 2] "The Domain Name System (DNS) translates human-readable hostnames into numeric IP addresses through a hierarchical, distributed resolution process. When a local resolver does not hold a cached record, it first queries a root name server to determine the authoritative Top-Level Domain (TLD) servers for the target zone. The TLD server directs the recursive resolver to the authoritative name servers that maintain the canonical zone records. The authoritative name server returns the requested A or AAAA record, which the recursive resolver caches locally for the duration specified by the Time-To-Live (TTL) header before delivering it to the operating system."
DNS queries recursive resolvers to retrieve authoritative zone records from root, TLD, and authoritative name servers.

[zero_shot_04] PASS [zero_shot_unseen_book] How does BGP prevent routing loops between autonomous systems?
  Latency: 0.665s
  Answer: [Ch. 2] "The Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) operates as the de facto path-vector routing protocol interconnecting autonomous systems (AS) across the global internet. Unlike interior gateway protocols that measure link metrics, BGP makes routing decisions based on network policies, path vectors, and administrative rule sets. BGP speakers exchange Network Layer Reachability Information (NLRI) via UPDATE messages containing the AS-Path attribute, which lists every autonomous system traversed. If a BGP router detects its own autonomous system number inside an incoming AS-Path attribute, it immediately discards the route to prevent routing loops."
BGP routers check incoming route paths and drop routes containing their own AS number to stop loops.

